/*
  screens_pgp.spthy -- the PGP pathway of the full interface: Alex's fingerprint verify + sign/attach-key
  + send (Screen A, PGP mode) and Blake's end-to-end inbox (Screen C), plus the REPLY leg that closes the
  ceremony (A13). Included by sender_screens.spthy ONLY when PATH_PGP is armed.

  Gating the pathway per profile is what buys the budget: with BOTH pathways compiled in, every profile
  carried the whole two-pathway, two-party surface and only ONE stressor fitted in 3 min. Compiled alone,
  a pathway is roughly half the surface, so a profile can arm a stressor TRIO again (as the legacy
  profiles do). A profile arms PATH_PGP and/or PATH_PW; the baseline arms both (it has no stressor).

  THE REPLY LEG (A13) IS ITS OWN LEVER -- PGP_REPLY -- and here is WHY it is opt-in rather than always-on.
  S3 (the recipient trio: sigma6+sigma8+sigma9+anxiety on Blake) already costs ~117s of the 180s budget
  with the pathway alone. Compiling the reply leg + the adversary-mediated C3 compare into EVERY PGP
  profile pushes S3 over 170s (measured: TIMEOUT). The leg is therefore a lever a profile ARMS, exactly
  like OUTCOME_PREMATURE / OUTCOME_CARELESS_MISROUTE: profiles that want the reciprocity story pay for it,
  the legacy trios do not. (screens_pgp.spthy is itself only INCLUDED in the taken PATH_PGP branch, so its
  inner #ifdefs are always processed in an ENTERED context -- never skipped through, which is the one
  thing tamarin's preprocessor cannot do.)

  WHAT THE LEG MODELS. The mock's composer carries a "Sign - attach my key" control (A13). Skipping it
  leaks NOTHING: it is not a secrecy control, it is a RECIPROCITY one. If Alex never attaches her key,
  Blake holds no key to encrypt a reply TO, so the conversation cannot come back -- the guide's
  completion story. Modelled as a PERFORMED confirm:
      Attentive       -> verify-self (fires only on <ownKey, ownKey>) -> Confirmed -> key attached
      Busy/Habituated -> autoapprove (clicks without looking)         -> Confirmed -> key attached
      Careless        -> dismiss     (swats the dialog away)          -> NO Confirmed -> NO key attached
  so the completion property (Sx_reply_completes) is an exists-trace, and its complement -- a DISMISSED
  confirm can never be followed by a reply -- is the all-traces Sx_reply_needs_attach. Neither is a
  secrecy claim: the dismissal path is a pure USABILITY failure and emits no Out.

  AND BLAKE'S TRUSTED-KEY COMPARE BECOMES ADVERSARY-MEDIATED (C3). It used to display a FIXED
  <'AliceKey','AliceKey'>, so Blake could never actually BE shown a substituted sender key -- his mistakes
  were mask-driven only. Under PGP_REPLY it reads In(senderKey) and displays <senderKey, 'Alex'>, exactly
  mirroring Alex's own compare (the OOB reference is the correspondent IDENTITY; tamperedness is DERIVED
  from the committed key, never from a genuine/tampered rule branch -- lesson 14). An Attentive Blake then
  fires only on <k,k> (i.e. only on the genuine 'Alex' key: he CATCHES the substitution), while a credulous
  mask accepts whatever was offered -> Mistake. The CONSEQUENCE is wired through the reply leg: a credulous
  Blake who trusts a substituted sender key encrypts his REPLY to the ADVERSARY's key and the reply leaves
  the boundary (Out). That reuses the existing Mistake(p,vid) outcome, which H_leak_routes in lemmas.spthy
  already carries as a disjunct -- NO new leak route, NO new disjunct, lemmas.spthy untouched.

  TRACTABILITY (each of these was the difference between a proof and a non-terminating search):
    * every Prompt is posed off a SHALLOW persistent anchor (!AlexScreen / BlakeInit), never off a deep
      linear chain.
    * an effect rule joins AT MOST ONE free-valued !StepData. The send joins the compare's recipient; the
      HIJACKED reply joins Blake's committed key. The GOOD reply joins !StepData(.., 'Alex') -- a GROUND
      pattern, so it costs nothing.
    * the attach is threaded as the LINEAR Confirmed token, never as a second free-valued !StepData.
    * the reply is bounded by a LINEAR ReplyToken minted at the decrypt: a persistent-only premise set
      plus Fr(~reply) would let the reply rules fire unboundedly.
*/

// ---- Screen A (PGP mode): fingerprint compare (verify), posed off the SHALLOW persistent !AlexScreen
// (as legacy IF_verify_fp is posed off !AlexSession). The recipient is the compare's committed key.
// Under PGP_REPLY the screen ALSO poses the A13 "Sign - attach my key" confirm. ----
#ifdef PGP_REPLY
rule IF_screen_pgp [color=#f2994a]:
    [ !AlexScreen('Alex', idA, subjId, sendId), In(offered), Fr(~vid), Fr(~cfgId), Fr(~sk) ]
  --[ UIStep('Alex','btn_verify_fp'), UIStep('Alex','btn_sign_attach'),
      KeyGenerated('Alex'), OwnSkGen('Alex', ~sk), PosePgpScreen('Alex') ]->
    [ Prompt('Alex', ~vid, 'compare'), !Demand('Alex', ~vid, 'compare'), !Displayed('Alex', ~vid, <offered, 'Blake'>),
      // A13 "Sign - attach my key": a PERFORMED confirm on Alex's OWN key. Attentive verify-self fires
      // only on <k,k>; Busy/Habituated autoapprove it; Careless DISMISSES it (-> no key attached -> no
      // reply is possible at all). 'confirm' carries no !Demands row, so this cannot trip sigma1/3/6/7,
      // and sigma8 needs TWO co-present confirms -- Alex has exactly one, so the screen stays
      // stressor-neutral for her.
      Prompt('Alex', ~cfgId, 'confirm'), !Demand('Alex', ~cfgId, 'confirm'), !Displayed('Alex', ~cfgId, <'AlexKey', 'AlexKey'>),
      AlexVerifying(idA, ~vid), !AlexAttachCtl(idA, ~cfgId) ]
#else
rule IF_screen_pgp [color=#f2994a]:
    [ !AlexScreen('Alex', idA, subjId, sendId), In(offered), Fr(~vid), Fr(~sk) ]
  --[ UIStep('Alex','btn_verify_fp'), KeyGenerated('Alex'), OwnSkGen('Alex', ~sk), PosePgpScreen('Alex') ]->
    [ Prompt('Alex', ~vid, 'compare'), !Demand('Alex', ~vid, 'compare'), !Displayed('Alex', ~vid, <offered, 'Blake'>),
      AlexVerifying(idA, ~vid) ]
#endif

restriction OncePosePgpScreen:
  "All p #i #j. PosePgpScreen(p)@i & PosePgpScreen(p)@j ==> #i = #j"

// good: decide handled (SecureFlag) + verify done (KeyChecked) + accepted 'Blake' + Send granted.
rule IF_send_pgp_screen_good [color=#1f6fd0]:
    [ SecureFlag(idA), !AlexScreen('Alex', idA, subjId, sendId), AlexVerifying(idA, vid),
      Granted('Alex', sendId), !KeyChecked('Alex', vid), !StepData('Alex', vid, 'Blake'), Fr(~m) ]
  --[ Send('Alex','PGP', ~m), Secret(~m), EncTo(~m,'Blake'), SentOK('Alex') ]->
    [ Mailbox('Blake', ~m), SentMsg(idA, ~m, subjId) ]

// leak (bad key): a credulous fingerprint compare accepted a non-'Blake' key -> Out. (Mistake at compare;
// carries EncTo shape so H_encto_shape prunes K(m).)
rule IF_send_pgp_screen_badkey [color=#c0392b]:
    [ SecureFlag(idA), !AlexScreen('Alex', idA, subjId, sendId), AlexVerifying(idA, vid),
      Granted('Alex', sendId), !KeyChecked('Alex', vid), !StepData('Alex', vid, rcpt), Fr(~m) ]
  --[ Send('Alex','PGP', ~m), Secret(~m), EncTo(~m, rcpt), Neq(rcpt,'Blake'), MisdirectedTo('Alex', rcpt) ]->
    [ Out(~m), SentMsg(idA, ~m, subjId) ]

// premature (OUTCOME_PREMATURE): Send granted BEFORE verify (GrantedUnchecked, NO KeyChecked join).
#ifdef OUTCOME_PREMATURE
rule IF_send_pgp_screen_premature [color=#c0392b]:
    [ SecureFlag(idA), !AlexScreen('Alex', idA, subjId, sendId), GrantedUnchecked('Alex', sendId), Fr(~m) ]
  --[ Send('Alex','PGP', ~m), Secret(~m), SendPremature('Alex'), MisdirectedTo('Alex','unverified') ]->
    [ Out(~m), SentMsg(idA, ~m, subjId) ]
#endif

// A13 EFFECT: the confirm was CONFIRMED (verify-self OR autoapprove -- both stand behind the attach; only
// the Careless DISMISS produces no Confirmed at all), so Alex's public key rides along with the message
// and Blake ends up holding a key he can encrypt a reply to. Joins the LINEAR Confirmed token, so it
// fires at most once and threads no second free-valued !StepData.
#ifdef PGP_REPLY
rule IF_attach_key [color=#1f6fd0]:
    [ !AlexAttachCtl(idA, cfgId), Confirmed('Alex', cfgId, mask) ]
  --[ KeyAttached('Alex', cfgId), AttachMask('Alex', mask) ]->
    [ !ReplyKeyAvail('Blake') ]
#endif

// ---- Screen C: recipient end-to-end inbox (Blake). Encryption/signature badges (C1/C2), trusted-key
// compare (C3), decrypt authorize, Reply (C6) + Forward (C7) + overflow (C8). Under PGP_REPLY the C3
// compare is ADVERSARY-MEDIATED (In(senderKey) against the OOB reference 'Alex'); otherwise it shows the
// fixed pair and is mask-driven only. ----
#ifdef PGP_REPLY
rule IF_screen_blake_pgp [color=#f2994a]:
    [ BlakeInit(idB), In(senderKey), Fr(~vid), Fr(~b1), Fr(~b2), Fr(~decId), Fr(~rplId), Fr(~fwdId), Fr(~ovfId), Fr(~sk) ]
  --[ UIStep('Blake','inbox'), KeyGenerated('Blake'), OwnSkGen('Blake', ~sk), Published('Blake'), PoseBlakePgp('Blake'),
      Posed('Blake', ~vid, 'compare'), Posed('Blake', ~decId, 'authorize'),
      Posed('Blake', ~fwdId, 'decide'), Posed('Blake', ~rplId, 'decide'), Posed('Blake', ~ovfId, 'decide'),
      CopresentClaim('Blake', ~vid, ~decId),
      CopresentDecideClaim('Blake', ~fwdId, ~rplId, ~ovfId) ]->
    [ // C3 shows the OFFERED sender key against the OOB reference (the correspondent identity 'Alex'): an
      // Attentive compare fires only on <k,k> and so CATCHES a substituted key; a credulous mask accepts
      // any offered key -> Mistake -> the reply gets encrypted to the adversary (IF_blake_reply_hijacked).
      Prompt('Blake', ~vid,   'compare'),   !Demand('Blake', ~vid,   'compare'),   !Displayed('Blake', ~vid, <senderKey, 'Alex'>),
      Prompt('Blake', ~decId, 'authorize'), !Demand('Blake', ~decId, 'authorize'),
      Prompt('Blake', ~fwdId, 'decide'),    !Demand('Blake', ~fwdId, 'decide'),
      !Demand('Blake', ~b1,    'confirm'),
      !Demand('Blake', ~b2,    'confirm'),
      !Demand('Blake', ~rplId, 'decide'),
      !Demand('Blake', ~ovfId, 'decide'),
      !Copresent('Blake'), !CopresentDecide('Blake'),
      !BlakePgpScreen(idB, ~decId, ~fwdId), !BlakeTrustCtl(idB, ~vid) ]
#else
rule IF_screen_blake_pgp [color=#f2994a]:
    [ BlakeInit(idB), Fr(~vid), Fr(~b1), Fr(~b2), Fr(~decId), Fr(~rplId), Fr(~fwdId), Fr(~ovfId), Fr(~sk) ]
  --[ UIStep('Blake','inbox'), KeyGenerated('Blake'), OwnSkGen('Blake', ~sk), Published('Blake'), PoseBlakePgp('Blake'),
      Posed('Blake', ~vid, 'compare'), Posed('Blake', ~decId, 'authorize'),
      Posed('Blake', ~fwdId, 'decide'), Posed('Blake', ~rplId, 'decide'), Posed('Blake', ~ovfId, 'decide'),
      CopresentClaim('Blake', ~vid, ~decId),
      CopresentDecideClaim('Blake', ~fwdId, ~rplId, ~ovfId) ]->
    [ // PERFORMED (a mask answers these -- they drive outcomes): trusted-key compare, decrypt, Forward.
      Prompt('Blake', ~vid,   'compare'),   !Demand('Blake', ~vid,   'compare'),   !Displayed('Blake', ~vid, <'AliceKey', 'AliceKey'>),
      Prompt('Blake', ~decId, 'authorize'), !Demand('Blake', ~decId, 'authorize'),
      Prompt('Blake', ~fwdId, 'decide'),    !Demand('Blake', ~fwdId, 'decide'),
      // EXPOSURE-ONLY (lesson 15a/15b): COUNTED by the density detectors, never PERFORMED -- they emit
      // !Demand WITHOUT a Prompt, so no mask answers them and they add ZERO branching.
      !Demand('Blake', ~b1,    'confirm'),
      !Demand('Blake', ~b2,    'confirm'),
      !Demand('Blake', ~rplId, 'decide'),
      !Demand('Blake', ~ovfId, 'decide'),
      // >=2 co-present controls (sigma2) and >=3 co-present DECIDES (sigma9) -- single markers, O(1).
      !Copresent('Blake'), !CopresentDecide('Blake'),
      !BlakePgpScreen(idB, ~decId, ~fwdId) ]
#endif

restriction OncePoseBlakePgp:
  "All p #i #j. PoseBlakePgp(p)@i & PoseBlakePgp(p)@j ==> #i = #j"

// decrypt (authorize) gates the read; a Fearful freeze on the authorize = the guide's abandonment case
// (safe-fail -- no Read). Reading holds m for a possible forward.
#ifdef PGP_REPLY
// ... and mints the ONE linear ReplyToken that BOUNDS the reply leg (see the header).
rule IF_blake_decrypt [color=#1f6fd0]:
    [ !BlakePgpScreen(idB, decId, fwdId), Granted('Blake', decId), Mailbox('Blake', m) ]
  --[ UIStep('Blake','btn_decrypt'), Read('Blake', m), ReadVia('Blake','PGP') ]->
    [ BlakeHasMsg(idB, m), ReplyToken(idB) ]
#else
rule IF_blake_decrypt [color=#1f6fd0]:
    [ !BlakePgpScreen(idB, decId, fwdId), Granted('Blake', decId), Mailbox('Blake', m) ]
  --[ UIStep('Blake','btn_decrypt'), Read('Blake', m), ReadVia('Blake','PGP') ]->
    [ BlakeHasMsg(idB, m) ]
#endif

// ---- the REPLY leg (C6). Blake can only reply ENCRYPTED, so he needs (a) a key from Alex -- the A13
// attach -- and (b) a key he TRUSTS: the one his C3 compare committed. WHICH key that is decides where the
// reply goes. Both rules burn the linear ReplyToken, so exactly one reply happens. ----
#ifdef PGP_REPLY
// good: Blake's compare committed the GENUINE sender ('Alex' -- the only key an Attentive compare can
// accept, since it fires only on <k,k>), and Alex attached her key. Delivered on the PRIVATE Mailbox --
// no Out, no new leak route. This is the RECIPROCITY property the user guide calls out.
rule IF_blake_reply_good [color=#1f6fd0]:
    [ ReplyToken(idB), !BlakeTrustCtl(idB, vid), !StepData('Blake', vid, 'Alex'), !ReplyKeyAvail('Blake'), Fr(~reply) ]
  --[ UIStep('Blake','btn_reply'), Reply('Blake', ~reply), Secret(~reply), ReplySentTo('Blake','Alex') ]->
    [ Mailbox('Alex', ~reply) ]

// HIJACKED: a credulous Blake trusted a SUBSTITUTED sender key (a Mistake at the C3 compare -- the key is
// adversary-supplied via In(senderKey)), so his reply is encrypted to the ADVERSARY's key and leaves the
// boundary. Joins the ONE free-valued !StepData (the committed key). Already covered by the Mistake(p,vid)
// disjunct of H_leak_routes -- no new disjunct.
rule IF_blake_reply_hijacked [color=#c0392b]:
    [ ReplyToken(idB), !BlakeTrustCtl(idB, vid), !StepData('Blake', vid, k), !ReplyKeyAvail('Blake'), Fr(~reply) ]
  --[ UIStep('Blake','btn_reply'), Reply('Blake', ~reply), Secret(~reply), NeqSender(k, 'Alex'),
      ReplyHijacked('Blake'), MisdirectedTo('Blake','substituted-sender-key') ]->
    [ Out(~reply) ]

restriction NeqSenderKey:
  "All k #i. NeqSender(k, 'Alex')@i ==> not (k = 'Alex')"

// the reply lands back on Alex's screen: the completion half of the ceremony. NOT labelled Read -- that
// label is reserved for the recipient reading m (the S*_read_only_blake lemmas pin it to Blake).
rule IF_alex_read_reply [color=#1f6fd0]:
    [ !AlexScreen('Alex', idA, subjId, sendId), Mailbox('Alex', r) ]
  --[ UIStep('Alex','read_reply'), ReplyRead('Alex', r) ]->
    [ ]
#endif

// Forward MISROUTE: Forward to a keyless third party re-sends m in the clear -> ForwardedOutside ->
// Out(m). The recipient-side breach of "only Alex and Blake know m" even when the sender was perfect.
#ifdef OUTCOME_CARELESS_MISROUTE
rule IF_blake_forward_misroute [color=#c0392b]:
    [ BlakeHasMsg(idB, m), DecisionMisrouted('Blake', fwdId) ]
  --[ ForwardedOutside('Blake'), MisdirectedTo('Blake','keyless-third-party') ]->
    [ Out(m) ]
#endif
